# Materialized ONCE from 1Password by chezmoi — the vault is the origin,
# this file is a cache. `create_` semantics: written only when MISSING,
# template never evaluated otherwise, so `op` (and the internet) are a
# bootstrap dependency, not a tick dependency.
# Rotation: delete this file, `chezmoi apply`, then
# `brew services restart beszel-agent` (the agent reads this at start only).
#
# ONE HONEST DIFFERENCE from every other create_ template in this repo: the
# vault is not the true origin of these two values, the HUB is. It mints
# them on first boot and shows them at /settings/tokens; you copy them into
# 1Password once. So unlike garage.env, wiping ~/Docker/beszel/data does NOT
# bring the same values back — it invalidates the vault copy, and you
# re-harvest. See beszel/RUNBOOK.md "Bootstrap".
#
# Read by the beszel-agent-launcher wrapper the Homebrew formula installs,
# which sources this file with `set -a` before exec'ing the agent. It is
# therefore SHELL syntax, not dotenv, so EVERY value is quoted — an
# unquoted assignment word-splits on the first space and bash tries to run
# the remainder as a command.
#
# GET THE TWO FIELDS THE RIGHT WAY ROUND. They are both opaque strings and
# the item's field names don't say which is which:
#     credential -> TOKEN, a UUID          (xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-...)
#     username   -> KEY,   an ssh pubkey   (ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza...)
# Swapped, the agent starts, fails to parse the token as a public key, exits
# 1, and launchd respawns it every 10s forever. `brew services list` shows
# `error` and the reason is only in ~/.cache/beszel/beszel-agent.log.

# Registration token — the agent presents this to enrol itself with the hub
# over an outbound WebSocket. A UNIVERSAL token: the same value enrols
# every server, the Pi over the tailnet.
# It must be marked PERSISTENT in the hub (Settings -> Tokens &
# Fingerprints): the default universal token dies after an hour and on
# every hub restart, which would leave this vaulted value useless by the
# time a new server needs it. Only registration needs it — an agent that has
# already enrolled keeps working regardless.
TOKEN="{{ onepasswordRead "op://Wolfe.Lab/beszel-agent/credential" }}"

# The hub's PUBLIC key — how the agent decides the thing on the other end
# really is our hub. Not secret (it is a public key, and the hub prints it
# in its own settings UI); it lives in the vault beside the token because
# the two are minted together by the same hub instance and are invalidated
# together, so keeping them in one item means one thing to re-harvest.
KEY="{{ onepasswordRead "op://Wolfe.Lab/beszel-agent/username" }}"

# Loopback only. In WebSocket mode the agent dials out and nothing ever
# connects IN, so binding the legacy listener to all interfaces would
# publish a metrics endpoint to the LAN for no one.
LISTEN=127.0.0.1:45876

{{ if eq .profile "macmini-node" -}}
# The hub is on this machine: localhost, so host monitoring depends on no
# name resolving (see compose.yaml).
HUB_URL=http://localhost:8090

# The two external disks, monitored alongside the root volume. This is the
# whole reason the agent is a HOST process: a container sees the Docker
# Desktop Linux VM's filesystem, in which /Volumes/Data1 does not exist at
# all. It is also the free-space check the backup scripts deliberately
# don't each carry — Data2 is where all of them write.
EXTRA_FILESYSTEMS=/Volumes/Data1,/Volumes/Data2

# Per-container CPU/memory/network, and an alert when a container stops.
# The socket is Docker's control plane — anything that can talk to it can
# start a container mounting the whole disk, so handing it to a CONTAINER
# converts one compromised container into a compromised host, and this lab
# does not do that. This agent is a different shape: a native process
# already running as tomwolfe, who owns the socket. Reading it grants the
# agent nothing it could not already do.
DOCKER_HOST=unix:///Users/tomwolfe/.docker/run/docker.sock
{{ end -}}
{{ if eq .profile "pi-node" -}}
# The hub is on the mini, reached by its MagicDNS name: resolved and routed
# by Tailscale itself, so monitoring depends only on the tailnet.
# Deliberately NOT beszel.ts.twolfe.dev — that path would add Netlify DNS
# and caddy to the chain for no gain.
HUB_URL=http://macmini.tailf823b8.ts.net:8090

# Per-container stats: the agent reads the socket as the login user, who
# is in the docker group — a native process that already owns the socket
# gains nothing from reading it (the mini's reasoning, same shape).
DOCKER_HOST=unix:///var/run/docker.sock
{{ end -}}
